Z||000000||msweldon||10-12-2000||03:33 PM||||I just bought a 3.4DOHC out of a 96' Monte Carlo and was wondering if you had any good sources information or documentation for the DOHC swap.<P>Is a kit needed from ACE?<P>What are your overall impressions?<P>Big sticky points?<P>Thanks in advance,<BR>Marc||||reg||1
Z||000001||william_thorniii||10-12-2000||03:36 PM||||You lucky dog... I have wanted to swap in the TDC for a while now.  How about you doccument it real well for me :~p<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
Z||000002||1FST2M6||10-12-2000||04:00 PM||||SWEET! mmmm.. DOCH.. gotta love that sound..  IMSA here we come! ||||reg||1
Z||000003||msweldon||10-12-2000||04:53 PM||||hey william,<P>take a look at <A HREF="http://www.copartfinder.com" TARGET=_blank>www.copartfinder.com</A> <P>I was originally thinking about doing a 3.6(3.4 overbored) with Ed Parks in AL and then putting forced induction on it in the future, similar to 1FST2M6's ongoing setup.  However, I just looked on CoPartFinder.com for about 30 minutes and found a 96' Monte Carlo that had been rolled just 1 week earlier and it was only 300 miles away so I couldn't pass it up.  I got the engine, wiring harness, and ECM for $1500 with 47K.<BR>Figure I can do the total swap for about 2000 w/ out a kit and help from 1FST2M6 and 3000 w/ a kit and help from 1FST2M6.(you reading this Trav?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">)<P>I've talked to Rich Cutter out at ACE and he "expectedly," highly recommends his 3.4DOHC kit.  It does seem like it would make the install a moderately difficult one to a moderately easy one.<P>I've emailed Chris West for recommendations on the swap with no reply yet.<P>I would like some feedback from members here on whether or not they would like me to do extensive documentation on the swap.  Most other DOHC swaps on the net are somewhat sparse.<P>marc||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||10-12-2000||05:57 PM||||Please do as much documentation as you can. I just aquired an '87 GT that may get this treatment.||||reg||14
Z||000005||Wannabe||10-12-2000||06:13 PM||||Please document! Sooner or later (probably later) my '87GT will be graced with a 3.4 DOHC too. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||10-12-2000||11:08 PM||||It's in process. We photograph some of it. It's a 94 DOHC into an 85 GT. Was going into my other 86SE but I scored a 3800SC and 4T60E so we're dropping it in a friends 85 GT. The SC is nearly done. Well, the DOHC is too.<P>The main issues are that the right hinge must be cut ( we're using a Commander Dickman Lid Lift (tm)) and the dogbone area must be removed on the right tower. Other than that, it fits fine. You can't get the rear plug wires off and the dip stick is in the back, and the throttle cable is too short and the Fiero clutch is too weak and A/C needs new hoses and PS pump needs to be gutted and half filled with PS fluid and there is no practical way for a dogbone (working on that)and the front mount bracket from a V6 Fiero needs to be notched for 84-87, but otherwise it fits fine......<P>The 95 and up DOHC is a bit different, but 90% is the same. <P>After that, it's the routing of hoses (coolant and heater). Get's a little weird.<P>After that, exhaust. This engine is a 94 so we used a left/front manifold on the back and made a Fiero style Y pipe with O2 bunge and air injection tubes (we're in CA).<P>After that, the wiring. It just takes time.<P>One issue with the 95 and up is that the ECM can't support manual trans. (Standing on soap box), if you like gremlins when it runs on automatic code, cool, but otherwise figure it will do strange things like stalling on a throttle closed snap and other stuff. More annoying than anything. Otherwise I suggest you find a 16149396 ECM and run it on that in batch fire mode. You can still buy the manual trans mem-cal from a 91-93 W car. (Off soapbox). Or of course, use the matching 4T60E. Then the lowend sucks.<P>That's about all I can offer. Well, it will take 100 hours in the end and suck another $500-750 beyond the cost of the engine.<P>Overall impressions? It's huge and fits. It looks good in Pontiac Red. It's a lot of work. It looks good and it's a lot of work. But it does look good.........<P>Terry<P><BR>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 10-12-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 10-12-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000007||Philphine||10-13-2000||01:43 AM||||if i'm able to do a v6 swap this is the engine i want. all info apreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000008||william_thorniii||10-13-2000||08:06 AM||||The resident "expert" of this swap from the fiero-list.org seems to be a gentleman that goes by MWS.  He has done the swap on his 86 or 87 GT and I have interrogated him several times about it in the past.  I will see if I can dig up either some of his old postings on the subject or get his e-mail addy so we can benefit from his knowledge.  <P>Has anyone seen that page by the guy at WVU with the supercharged TDC Fiero?  Talk about a beautiful engine!  I had better shut up before I drool too much on the keyboard.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000009||1FST2M6||10-13-2000||08:18 AM||||i'm all over it!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  anything you need... ill see what i can conjure up. i've got a HUGE shop at my disposal full of tools if either one of us doens't have a tool. and access to 6 ASE Master Tech that all live with in  10 minutes from me/you. LETS DO THIS! Need a turbo? i've got 3 now! only gunna use one though... (1, TD-06-16g and 2 T-28 super60(not being used))  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> you need two! i've got intercooler cores too!<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 10-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||william_thorniii||10-13-2000||09:27 AM||||1fast,<P>You keep talking like that and I am coming down for a week.  <P>I just got off of the phone with a boneyard in Indiana.  I thought I had found a true pot of gold.  Few folks know that in 1992 some select Lumina Z-34's were outfitted with a TDC and a 5-spd manual transmission.  These cars are EXTREMELY rare.  I have been looking for one since 95 and only heard of two.  Anyway... I found one in a bone yard in Indiana.  Unfortunately it was sold to them almost 2 months ago, and it is not gone.  <P>Oh well... better luck next time. <P>Serously though 2m6, if you all are going to be doing work on weekends, I might make the drive down from Ohio... I guess I would need to run it by the wife though.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000011||pollock||10-13-2000||09:49 AM||||Terry, <P>that's the best summary i've heard yet about this.  Couple questions, if you don't mind.<P>What's a mem-cal?<P>You can't get the rear plug wires off!!??  So you can't change plugs in-car?  Need a firewall access panel, like a van?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||msweldon||10-13-2000||11:05 AM||||Thanks for the info Terry, I've already begun documentation and will start a web page documenting decisions and parts I'm pre-purchasing e.g. kits, tools, etc...<P>1FST2M6: Thanks for the help, I was kinda hoping you'd be excited about it.  We've still got to hook up and meet someday soon, and we only live 5 miles from each other.  Trav, do you think the HalTech ECU will be able to run the 96' DOHC with the 5spd tranny w/out the squirrely problems that Terry mentioned? I also presume the Haltech is OBD II compliant.  Were would you want to do the swap? Your place? Engineered.net?  My home garage is way too small and to suburbian.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>William: I don't plan on starting the swap until late this year or early next year, so I'm using now until then to plan and document as much as possible(the curse of a software engineer).  If you want to wait until after I(we) get the swap done I'll have all of the gotchas e.g. ECM, Cutting, mounts documented and noted, to help you out.<P>I also found the same Z34 TDC 5spd in Indiana, stripped to the frame though.  Those 284 tranny's, If you can find one, go for around $2K and there are no rebuild kits for them.<P>The ACE kit has a dog bone replacement that keeps the stock fiero dog-bone location as well as hoses, cruise and throttle cables, mounts, gauge cluster mod, wiring harness mod and much more.  He also sells a transmission kit that upgrades the VSS unit to an electronic pulse unit. I'm still debating on whether or not the kit is worth the cost.<P>I'm hoping I can unlock the original specs (285hp/~255torque) of this engine with the Haltech programmable ECM, we'll have to see.<P>So far this is what I've purchased/planned:<BR>(purchased)<BR>96' DOHC w/ ECM $1500<BR>Centerforce Stage II $300<P>(planned)<BR>ACE 3.4 Kit(maybe) $1700<BR>ACE Tranny Kit(maybe) $200<BR>Haltech ECU(maybe)  $1000<BR>Upgraded Fuel pump $250<BR>much, much, more.... $$$<P><BR>Hey 1FST2M6, you got some time next week to meet..<P>Thanks for all the feedback everyone,<BR>keep it coming,<BR>Marc<BR><p>[This message has been edited by msweldon (edited 10-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||1FST2M6||10-13-2000||11:13 AM||||you can batch fire with the E6..  and if we have issue with the Haltech... a Link engine management system will work... we can limp it to the shop and FLOG it on the dyno till it works right! but i'd stay away from the center force... explain more when i see ya.<P>------------------<BR>Shhh.. Top Secret! <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstcheeto_2.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 10-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||msweldon||10-13-2000||11:15 AM||||Hey Terry,<P>What "exactly" happens when you run the ECM in Batch Fire Mode?<P>thanks,<BR>marc||||reg||1
Z||000015||terryk||10-13-2000||01:34 PM||||The injectors fires three at a time on each bank rather than sequentially (only one injector fired as needed). GM sequentials switch to batch fire above 2500-3000 depending on the car anyway.<P>Sequential improves idle and low RPM performance a bit. The emissions are affected a bit too.<P>91-93 DOHC were batch fire and 94 and up were sequential. ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Wannabe||10-13-2000||01:51 PM||||Isn't there a way to do away with the power steering pump? A few sources mention plugging  it up, but is sounds like useless drag and weight if left on. <P>The 284 manual trans that came in the w-bodies was know to have serious reliablity problems. Probably more practical to stick with the 282.<P>How is the Haltech programmed, and yeah, people with the 3.4 dohc already installed, how do you change plugs?||||reg||1
Z||000017||william_thorniii||10-13-2000||02:54 PM||||The only reason that the 284 was plagued with problems was because it was forced to withstand 215 bhp day in and day out.  I would imagine that the 282's would be significantly worse considering that is beyound their design intent.  <P>If you believe GM folklore (this one I do by the way), the transmission is what made GM limit the TDC to 215 bhp anyway.  If you think about it it makes some sense... they were cranking 185 bhp out of the Quad 4.  so out of another litre of displacement, they could only find 30 ponies?  Seems like they might have screwed around with the ECM to limit the specific output.  Kind of like they did to the Chevy 350's in the mid 70's.  <P>This is just my opinion though...<P>MSWELDON&gt;&gt;<P>One question on that Lumina in Indiana... did you find out how much they were asking for it when you called?<P><BR>William T. Thorn, III<BR>This is my kind of thread!||||reg||3
Z||000018||msweldon||10-13-2000||03:14 PM||||William: the 284 was the same as the 282 except it had a stronger casing and internal webbing.  It was a special edition of the 282 specifically for the 3.4TDC's and was rated up to ~280ft/lbs torque.  It also had a slightly different gearing  setup for the 3.4tdc.  <BR>I chose not to locate a 284 because, (although they are a slightly better made than the 282)<P>1.) 282's are readily available.<BR>2.) There are no rebuild kits available for the 284.(if it goes, time to locate another one)<BR>3.) GM nor Jasper sell them nor parts for the 284<BR>4.) You can have a blown 284 remanufactured through a GM dealer for about 2K. So 2k for the tranny + 2K for a potential rebuild, ick!! I'll just drive carefully.<P>If you want to know everything about the 3.4 and its history goto.   <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/ca2/34Performance" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/ca2/34Performance</A> <P>The engine was emission certified 281hp at the crank but gm coudn't make an auto tranny strong enough to hold it, so it was detuned via the ECM and the Fuel Pump.<P>I'm no guru, everything I've stated has come from sources on the net and in person.<P>thanks,<BR>marc||||reg||1
Z||000019||1FST2M6||10-13-2000||03:18 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.warmotorsport.com/linkcpu.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.warmotorsport.com/linkcpu.html</A> <P>anything you can't do with a Haltec you can do with this!!! guaranteed! shop has 6 or so of these on various race cars and 2 street cars! ||||reg||1
Z||000020||terryk||10-13-2000||05:40 PM||||The pump takes .0000000000005HP when the vanes are removed. A simple solution. ||||reg||1
Z||000021||terryk||10-13-2000||05:43 PM||||.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 10-13-2000).]||||reg||12
Z||000022||terryk||10-13-2000||07:15 PM||||Pollack,<P>On the plugs, just drop the rear of the cradle a few inches and the plugs swing clear of the hood.<P>Mem-cal is what we call the "prom" or "chip" on the Fiero, but a mem-cal includes the prom, knock sensor filter and fuel backup chips. Memory calibrator.....||||reg||1
Z||000023||lowCG||10-13-2000||09:46 PM||||Wow,alot of interest going on about the 3.4 swap!<BR>I have about 4000 miles on mine now,and here is what I can recommend from my experience.<BR>The throttle cable from the V6 fiero can be used by grinding down whatever is on the end of it and slipping a small piece of tubing that's slotted on one side.<BR>The shifter cable bracket needs to be moved by making a spacer 1" thick to go between it and the bellhousing.<BR>The motor mounts I used were Chris West's urethane units front and back,used oem under the crank pulley.No bad vibrations with these,just right.<BR>tTe wiring harness was wrapped up in tape very carefully after sorting all the wires out.I measured around and wound up running the wire bundle from along the starter solenoid over into the passenger's corner of the engine compartment,putting the ECM in the front-wheelhouse area,strapped to a plate which I bolted to the web of the frame.<BR>I used an S-10,2.8 water outlet,bolted to the bottom of the 2.8 Fiero water neck.This allowed me to just plug the extra  water lines,and now a short length of hose connects the 3.4 neck to the Fiero one,with another hose going right down to the hard line on the car*(drivers side).The pass. side just connects to the water pump.<BR>I think you figured out the PS pump.<BR>You also need to move the cables over where they exit through the firewall,but you only need to make one more hole,then shift the pair ove,plugging the one unused hole.<BR>The intake was accomplished by a 3",45 Degree pvc elbow and some rubber patch pieces from Home Depot.<BR>The shift solenoids require the use of some fairly heavy resistors,with power applied.I placed these outside the wire bundle as they create alot of heat.I don't recall the value right now.<BR>The flywheel is stock Fiero,with the counter weight ground off and nuetral balanced.<BR>I'm now using a stock clutch,but I don't want to talk about that one right now until I cool off a little more and get some more info as to why my first one failed.<BR>My coil pack was located on the passenger side on the Monte Carcass,so it had to be relocated to the intake manifold,next to the throttle body.I did this without having to drop the motor again by swapping two of the wires that went to the same coil.<BR>Terry,how large is your exhaust after the merge?<BR>Maybe I can muster up some pictures here if I can find my camera after the move! <BR>Almost forgot,I'm using an electronic fuel controller made by Split Second that allows changes to the mixture at different ranges and situations,it made the car driveable again after the swap,but cost me $500.I had fun dialing it in one day back in Dearborn chasing a prototype FR500 stang down the freeway. <p>[This message has been edited by lowCG (edited 10-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000024||Dan86gt3.8||10-15-2000||05:48 PM||||250 for a fuel pump?  With my 3.8 you just used some vette fuel pump that can put out some crazy amount of pressure.  It was outargeous at $75 I paid for it.  but it works fine and the engine has never "fuel starved"<P>also on the 3.8 we just took off the power steering pump and got a shorter belt and re-routed it...  anyone tried that?||||reg||1
Z||000025||terryk||10-15-2000||10:26 PM||||I couldn't figure out a way to re-route the belt without the PS pump on the Series I SC. I just gutted it.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Wannabe||10-15-2000||11:46 PM||||I like the re-routing idea. Hard to say how well it will work on the TDC without having a close look. There isn't a TDC sitting in my garage. YET!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>How good are the exhaust manifolds an crossover on the 3.4 DOHC? ||||reg||1
Z||000027||terryk||10-16-2000||01:03 AM||||The PS is at the top of DOHC. Can't imagine how that would work with no pump. I guess anything is possible. Seems like a lot of effort compared to two minutes of work to open the pump and remove the vanes.||||reg||1
Z||000028||olympic||10-16-2000||02:18 AM||||  Yes, I also read somewhere that the TDC was capable of almost 300hp but was detuned to 215hp because turbo hydromatic would not let GM use one of their trannys with more than 220 hp.<P>  Same goes for the Taurus SHO.  Yamaha built the engine to produce 300hp but the MTX 5 speed had reliability problems so the engine was choked with a small MAF sensor, constrictive exhaust system etc, to lower its output to 220 hp.||||reg||1
Z||000029||msweldon||10-16-2000||09:35 AM||||Thanks for the tip on the fuel pump, what model corvette was it from?<P>Of all the 3.4 swaps out there, I haven't heard of anyone really attempting to get the full potential ~275 horses out of the engine w/out some sort of forced induction.  <P>The engine is sitting on a crate 300 miles away, so until I truck it up here I can't give you any opinions on the exhaust manifolds.  However from what I've heard the manifolds flow very well for stock, similar to the stock fiero.<P>David Glubrich(sp?) replaced his 3.4 PS pump pulley with an Idler Pulley from a vw rabbit.<P>thanks for all the feed back,<BR>marc||||reg||1
Z||000030||william_thorniii||10-16-2000||10:48 AM||||ms,<P>THX for the info on the 284.  I agree that it is a dangerous proposition, but if one were to be able to acheieve the motor's potential... it might be necessary.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000031||Wannabe||10-16-2000||02:45 PM||||Considering that the 282 has been installed behind v8's and 3.8SC's with reportedly no problems, then life with the 3.4 DOHC should be easy, since the 3.4 really can't be considered a high torque motor like the others.<P>As far as realizing the engine's full potential, supposedly, the ECM resticts fuel delivery above 5000 rpm in order to limit the engine's output and save the weak kneed auto trans from vaporizing. The story goes that, if the fuel curve can be richened at the top end of the rev range, the engine will produce in the neighborhood of 260-270HP without any other mods. You'd think that if it was really so easy, some enterprising chip maker would have caught on by now and created one of the most surprising performance packages around. Beyond that, there is a slick Rotrex supercharger kit that's being introduce for the 3.4 DOHC that sounds very cool. It just might fit in place of the ps pump  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000032||terryk||10-16-2000||05:33 PM||||There is some wishful thinking there.<P>There is torque management for the shifts (spark retard), but leaning the mixture out at 5000 RPM to save the trans at the expense of the pistons and valves is not realistic.<P>I'll bet dollars to donuts the "detuning" (if it's true and one GM engineer disagrees) was done with the camshafts and not in the ECM calibrations. GM does enrichen the fuel at times to limit power and cool the cylinders. Nothing new there.<P>No doubt a *version* of the DOHC produced 280+HP, but I seriously doubt the production engine can without forced induction.||||reg||1
Z||000033||zeusrekning||10-16-2000||08:18 PM||||i am guessing i should never consider doing this swap? 1fst2m6 where in georgia are you located? opps forgate capitols . OK THIS WILL MAKE UP FOR IT? . I LIVE RIGHT OFF I-385 AND HIWAY 49 about 30 miles south of greenville. wHAT IS THE BEST SWAP TO DO IF <BR>1.You are broke.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>2.you engine has 220,000+++++ miles<BR>3.You are broke.<BR>4. want a little more power.<BR>5.You are broke<BR>6.Want to do the job yourself?<BR>And 7 i am broke because i'm building a 450-500h.p. (455) firebird. <BR>how do you have access to a dyno. email me if you have time. zeusrekning@hotmail.com<BR>sorry everyone else just felt like typing.<P>How are you all doing. <BR>is the 2.8 the easiest swap to do other than a newer 2.5<BR>ok i'll stop now.  <p>[This message has been edited by zeusrekning (edited 10-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000034||Dan86gt3.8||10-16-2000||09:21 PM||||ok the part-number on that fuel pump is  g/m# 25163464.  If I rember right it was alott cheaper at the parts stores.  If you do that you are supposed to ask for a 92-96 vette or 94 impala.  This is the one I used on my series 2 3.8 (n/a).||||reg||1
Z||000035||Mark O'Hara||10-16-2000||10:53 PM||||Man am I glad I found this place. I have been working and thinking and stumped and have more questions now than before but I know I've found my fellow 3.4 lovers. Some people like Glubrecht and Smith (MWS) just jumped in and did it. I have been trying to puzzle it out on the side with two jobs and Go-Kart racing but I really have no excuse.<BR>I should be driving it now. I am going to convert it to a GM 4T65E so that the performance is a little better than with the three speed but there are so many unanswered questions. Is ther a sipercharger that fits the power steering spot? (PLEASE PLEASE PLEASE PLEASE) If not thanks for the hint about removinf the vanes. I just have the output piped back into the return. This will be much better. I guess the idea of the resisters on the trany shift control lines is to fool the new PCM into thinking that the tranny is auto not manual or the old tranny? How do you take care of the shift position switch? Must therre be a fill neck at hte top of the engine? Couldn't you fill at the radiator and bleed at the top of the engine with the bleeder valve at the back cooling hose connection near the throttle body? Sorry I've taken so much space. Thanks in advance for being here in the first place and for any info you can give me.<P>Irish||||reg||7
Z||000036||lowCG||10-16-2000||11:30 PM||||I've leaned out the mixture considerably from the stock setting,I assume that the short exhaust has something to do with it,but just I can't imagine how being richer is going to unleash another 60 hp!||||reg||1
Z||000037||terryk||10-16-2000||11:36 PM||||Let us know how it works.||||reg||1
Z||000038||Dan86gt3.8||10-17-2000||02:19 AM||||Man Mark O hara whats up?  I thought there could only be one fool crazy enough to swap engines in fieros and race karts.  When I come home on the weekends I usually have to balance my time inbetween tinkering with the fiero and getting the karts ready to race.  But being in indiana shouldn't your season almost be up.  I know it was cold as hell at our last race.  I could only imagine how cold it is up there?  What kind of karts do you run?  Sprint/dirt? Shiters/yamahas?  I got one last race and we got a 135 tkm reed vlave thats haulin butt!  I plan to have lots of fun next saturday night at the last race.  <BR>p.s.- I do not have a fill neck on mine.  I don't know how the 3.4 is setup but on the thermostat housing on a 3.8 there is a brass screw.  Wee took mine out and put in a radiator drian vlave.  so whenever you need to bleed the cooling lines you can do it from there or park on a hill or jack up the front and fill the system and get the air out like that.  and when you are through just screw the wing nut back on.||||reg||1
Z||000039||Wannabe||10-17-2000||08:01 AM||||Agreed on the mixture thing. It was probably just a story deamed up by a power starved TDC owner. I had a Cutlass with one of those engines....the auto trans, 3600lb curb weight and soft bottom end didn't do much for performance. Ram Air Tech  <A HREF="http://www.ramairtech.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ramairtech.com/</A>  is developing a supercharger for these engines in the W-body cars. It should be adaptable some way somehow to for Fiero installs.||||reg||1
